**`assume()` states the invariant to the optimizer; it does not trap a violation.**

`r300_validate_fragment_shader` guards on `r300->fs.state` being non-NULL, so the CSO pointer is not unconditionally valid across this file. With `assume()`, a NULL `fs_state`/`shader` here is undefined behavior the compiler may exploit (branch elimination on the later `deriv_fs &&` test at line 395), rather than a diagnosable dereference. Pair it with `assert()` so a debug build fails at the violation site.

<details>
<summary>Proposed guard</summary>

```diff
     struct r300_vertex_shader *vs_state = r300_vs(r300);
     struct r300_fragment_shader *fs_state = r300_fs(r300);
+    assert(vs_state && vs_state->shader);
+    assert(fs_state && fs_state->shader);
     assume(vs_state && vs_state->shader);
     assume(fs_state && fs_state->shader);
```
</details>

As per coding guidelines, "assertions guard impossible internal states".

